WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.010
Film Courage: …That being said do you think
it’s easier to work on your own or to have

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.010
collaborators?

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.779
James Kicklighter, Filmmaker: I think that
it’s always easier to work on your own but

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:09.540
it’s never better.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:16.550
I think that I’m so skeptical of anybody
who says there are a “Writer, Producer,

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:17.550
Actor, Editor.”

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.620
They are not good at all those jobs.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.060
There are one or two that they are good at.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.530
And I think that when people say they are
multi-hyphenates of all those things, they

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:29.150
usually are not good at any of them.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.980
Be one or two things and be good at them,
that will make your work better.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:41.550
I can live in isolation and technically can
I direct, edit, produce, shoot…technically

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.829
I could.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:46.050
I know how to do all of those things.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.850
But I know what I’m best at.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:56.579
I’m a good director, I’m a good editor
and so I will sometimes do those two things.

00:00:56.579 --> 00:00:59.910
And even when I’m editing work that I’ve
directed, if I think that I’m not being

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:07.580
disciplined enough or if I’m not being judicious
or I’m lost in the woods, I’ll always

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.240
bring in a second editor because I can’t
be precious about it in my own work because

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.520
that looks poorly on me as a director, right?

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.670
So you have to be cognizant about those things.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.220
So could you work on your own and do your
own thing?

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.509
Sure you can but your work is going to suffer.

00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:26.600
I think most people who do that there work
does suffer.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.520
There are very few examples of people who
are successful that I don’t think most people

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.600
can pull that off.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.410
Film Courage: But in the middle let’s say.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.070
Starting out maybe they can’t afford…

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.170
James: Oh yes, when you are starting out you
have to.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:41.909
Now that’s different.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:43.759
If you are starting out that’s different.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:49.000
When I first started out I was producer, director,
all of those things.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:58.591
But if you want to make that transition to
the middle (the middle…best place!) you

00:01:58.591 --> 00:02:00.090
can’t do that anymore.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.700
You have to set that aside.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.899
You have to start leaning in on and working
with people who are professionals in those

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:09.140
areas.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.650
I don’t think you would go to (using the
accounting example) a CPA who is also a chef

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.950
who is also a Uber driver.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.459
You would probably think that one of those
things they may not be very good at.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:22.750
It’s the same thing with film.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.430
Film Courage: And maybe the need to hire out
means you are entering the middle.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.430
James: Right.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.581
Well I think it also shows that you are entering
a different mindset because there are two

00:02:32.581 --> 00:02:34.569
kinds of filmmakers.

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:38.670
There is the professional filmmaker which
I am lucky to be now.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.519
And there are the ones who do it for themselves.

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:45.250
They just kind of make it and they have their
regular job and that’s what they do, that’s

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.250
totally fine.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.690
Neither one of those are bad or wrong.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.459
But if you are going to be a film professional,
you’ve got to work with other people because

00:02:54.459 --> 00:03:01.510
they are trained specifically in specialty
areas that you are not.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:07.769
And if I’m trying to do all of those things,
I’m invariably going to drop one of those

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:15.050
balls or not do one of those things well because
I am not trained.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:21.909
I am not a lawyer who took the Bar and passed
it.

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:29.290
I am not going to write my own contract because
I took an entertainment law class in college.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.190
That does not make me a lawyer.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:37.630
That means that I might understand basic verbalogies
and structures but I’m not going to have

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:44.790
myself write my own contract with the company
to direct a film because I know that.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:49.010
That doesn’t make me good at it, that means
I know something about it.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:54.299
I will hire the lawyer that has been trained
in entertainment law that passed the bar that

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:59.660
is practicing in multiple states to do my
negotiations for me.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.709
It’s the same thing with hiring a crew.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:03.709
Would you hire a lawyer?

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:04.720
Do you want to do your own legal?

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.049
I don’t think so.

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:07.200
I don’t want to do that.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.360
I’m not trained in it.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.240
I’ll screw me over in the end.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:12.499
Same thing will happen if you try to do everything
yourself.

